---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { ISSUES, REPO } from "../consts";

const toc = [
  ["before", "Before you start"],
  ["docker", "Install with Docker"],
  ["source", "Run from source"],
  ["first-run", "First run"],
  ["connect", "Connect your services"],
  ["folders", "Folders and hardlinks"],
  ["playback", "Playback"],
  ["config", "Configuration"],
  ["backups", "Backups and updates"],
  ["help", "Getting help"],
];

const env = [
  ["MOKA_CONFIG_DIR", "./config", "Database, caches and the secret key. /config in Docker."],
  ["MOKA_PORT", "8484", "Port for the web app and the API."],
  ["MOKA_HOST", "0.0.0.0", "Address to listen on."],
  ["MOKA_DATABASE_URL", "SQLite in the config folder", "Only if you want the database somewhere else."],
  ["MOKA_SECRET_KEY", "secret.key in the config folder", "Base64, 32 bytes. Encrypts stored passwords and API keys."],
];
---

<Base
  title="Docs · Moka"
  description="Install Moka with Docker or from source, connect your download client, indexers and library, and set it up next to the apps you already run."
  page="docs"
>
  <div class="wrap docs">
    <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="toc-title">On this page</p>
      <ol>{toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
    </aside>

    <article class="doc">
      <header class="doc-head">
        <h1>Docs</h1>
        <p>
          How to install Moka, connect it to your services and keep it running. This page follows the code on the
          <code>main</code> branch.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section id="before">
        <h2>Before you start</h2>
        <p class="note">
          <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>
            Moka is a development preview. There are no releases yet, and settings or the database can change between
            commits. Point it at a copy of your media, or at folders you can afford to have reorganised.
          </span>
        </p>
        <p>You will need:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A machine that stays on: a NAS, a mini PC, a home server. Docker makes it easiest.</li>
          <li>A download client: qBittorrent or Transmission for torrents, SABnzbd for Usenet.</li>
          <li>One or more Torznab or Newznab indexers, added directly or through Prowlarr.</li>
          <li>Folders for your downloads and your library, ideally on the same disk.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Moka ships with no content sources. What it can find depends entirely on what you connect.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="docker">
        <h2>Install with Docker</h2>
        <p>There is no published image yet, so you build it from the repository. The build takes a few minutes.</p>
        <pre><code>git clone {REPO}.git
cd Moka
cp docker-compose.example.yml docker-compose.yml
<span class="c"># set your media folder under volumes, then:</span>
docker compose up -d --build</code></pre>
        <p>The example mounts two folders:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>./config</code> as <code>/config</code>: the database, caches and the secret key.</li>
          <li>
            Your media folder as <code>/data</code>, holding both downloads and the library (see
            <a href="#folders">Folders and hardlinks</a>).
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Then open <code>http://your-server:8484</code>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="source">
        <h2>Run from source</h2>
        <p>
          You need Rust (stable), Node.js 22 or newer with pnpm, and FFmpeg for playback. The web app is built into the
          server binary, so build it first.
        </p>
        <pre><code>pnpm --dir web install
pnpm --dir web build
cargo run --release -p moka-server</code></pre>
        <p>Moka listens on <code>http://localhost:8484</code> and keeps its data in <code>./config</code>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="first-run">
        <h2>First run</h2>
        <p>
          On the first visit Moka asks you to create an account. That first account is the admin. Moka starts empty, so
          the catalog works right away but nothing can be downloaded until you connect your services.
        </p>
        <p>
          Add people for your household or friends under <strong>Settings → People</strong>, and choose what each one
          may do: request, have requests approved automatically, manage requests or manage the library.
          <strong>Settings → Request limit</strong> caps how much each person can request in a period.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="connect">
        <h2>Connect your services</h2>
        <dl class="steps">
          <div>
            <dt>Download client</dt>
            <dd>
              <strong>Connections → Download clients.</strong> Add qBittorrent, Transmission or SABnzbd with its address
              and login. Moka adds its downloads under their own category, <code>moka</code> by default.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Indexers</dt>
            <dd>
              <strong>Connections → Indexers.</strong> Paste a Torznab or Newznab address with its API key, or import
              every indexer you already have in Prowlarr at once. Moka searches all of them together.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Library</dt>
            <dd>
              <strong>Settings → Library.</strong> Choose a films folder and a shows folder. If they already hold
              titles, scan them and Moka brings them in as they are.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Quality</dt>
            <dd>
              <strong>Settings → Quality.</strong> List the releases you accept, best first. Moka downloads the first
              acceptable one it finds and upgrades it until it reaches the point where you told it to stop.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Jellyfin</dt>
            <dd>
              <strong>Connections → Jellyfin.</strong> Moka tells Jellyfin about new files right after import and brings
              back what people watched there. If the two see your media under different paths, map one to the other.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>People and requests</dt>
            <dd>
              <strong>Users.</strong> Add the people who use Moka and choose what each may do: request films, shows or
              everything, with or without approval, and their own limit for films and for shows (left empty, the
              global limit under <strong>Settings</strong> applies).
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Discord bot</dt>
            <dd>
              Run <a href="https://github.com/openVESSL/Anchorr">Anchorr</a> next to Moka. In Anchorr, turn on
              <strong>System → Experimental → Use Moka</strong>, then connect Moka under <strong>Connections → Moka</strong>
              with an admin account. Give each person their Discord ID under <strong>Users</strong> in Moka, and they
              request from Discord in their own name and get a message when their title is ready.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Radarr</dt>
            <dd>
              <strong>Connections → Radarr.</strong> Let Radarr do the searching and downloading: approved film requests
              go to Radarr, and every film it has appears in Moka to watch.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Other apps</dt>
            <dd>
              <strong>Connections → API keys.</strong> Apps that talk to Radarr or Sonarr, such as indexer managers,
              request apps and subtitle apps, can use Moka's address with a key from here.
              <strong>Connections → Notifications</strong> tells Discord or any service that accepts JSON when
              something is requested, ready or stuck, and calls a subtitle app's webhook after each import.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section id="folders">
        <h2>Folders and hardlinks</h2>
        <p>
          Keep downloads and the library on the same disk, and in Docker inside the same mount. Then an import is a
          hardlink: instant, and it takes no extra space while the download keeps seeding. Across disks Moka copies
          instead. It never moves or deletes the downloaded file.
        </p>
        <pre><code>/srv/media/
  downloads/   <span class="c"># your download client saves here</span>
  movies/      <span class="c"># films folder in Settings → Library</span>
  shows/       <span class="c"># shows folder in Settings → Library</span></code></pre>
        <p>
          Films are filed as <code>Title (Year) {"{tmdb-ID}"}</code> and shows as
          <code>Show (Year) {"{tmdb-ID}"}/Season 01</code>, names other media servers read without trouble.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="playback">
        <h2>Playback</h2>
        <p>
          Files the browser can play are sent as they are. Others are repackaged or converted on the fly with FFmpeg.
          Subtitles inside the file or next to it are picked up, styled ones included.
        </p>
        <p>
          Conversion is faster on a graphics card. Pick it under <strong>Settings → Playback</strong>: NVIDIA (NVENC),
          Intel or AMD on Linux (VAAPI), or Intel Quick Sync. If the card fails, Moka falls back to the processor. In
          Docker the card has to be passed to the container; the compose example shows how for each kind.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="config">
        <h2>Configuration</h2>
        <p>Everything else is set in the web app. These environment variables cover what has to be known at start:</p>
        <div class="table">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                env.map(([name, def, what]) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>{name}</code></td>
                    <td>{def}</td>
                    <td>{what}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="backups">
        <h2>Backups and updates</h2>
        <p>
          Back up the config folder, and keep <code>moka.db</code> and <code>secret.key</code> together. Without the key,
          stored indexer keys and passwords cannot be read and have to be entered again.
        </p>
        <p>To update a Docker install, pull and rebuild:</p>
        <pre><code>git pull
docker compose up -d --build</code></pre>
        <p>Moka upgrades its database on start and keeps a copy of the previous one next to it.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="help">
        <h2>Getting help</h2>
        <p>
          Questions, ideas and bug reports go to the <a href={ISSUES}>issues on GitHub</a>. Say what you tried, what you
          expected and what happened; real setups help more than anything else right now.
        </p>
        <p>
          Found a security problem? Please do not open a public issue. Use <strong>Security → Report a vulnerability</strong>
          on the <a href={REPO}>repository</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .docs {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem 4rem;
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
  }

  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .docs {
      grid-template-columns: 13rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .toc {
      position: sticky;
      top: 6rem;
      align-self: start;
    }
  }

  .toc-title {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    color: var(--faint);
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .toc ol {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.1rem;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .toc a {
    display: block;
    padding: 0.3rem 0 0.3rem 0.9rem;
    margin-left: -1px;
    border-left: 1px solid transparent;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.925rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .toc a:hover {
    color: var(--fg);
    border-left-color: var(--line-strong);
  }

  @media (max-width: 59.99rem) {
    .toc {
      padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    }

    .toc ol {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(9rem, 1fr));
      border-left: 0;
    }

    .toc a {
      padding-left: 0;
      border-left: 0;
    }
  }

  .doc {
    max-width: 44rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .doc-head {
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 1.6rem + 2.4vw, 3.2rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
  }

  .doc-head p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding-block: 2.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    color: #d4d4d8;
    font-size: 1.025rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 750;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--fg);
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }

  strong {
    color: var(--fg);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  ul li::marker {
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  .note {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    background: var(--raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  .note .dot {
    background: var(--requested);
    transform: translateY(-0.1em);
  }

  .steps {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
  }

  .steps > div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.2rem 1.5rem;
    padding-block: 0.9rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .steps > div:first-child {
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .steps > div {
      grid-template-columns: 9rem 1fr;
    }
  }

  dt {
    font-weight: 650;
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .table {
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.925rem;
  }

  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  tbody tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  th {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--muted);
    background: var(--raised);
  }

  td:nth-child(2) {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
